# infra/.env.example — copy to infra/.env and fill in, then `docker compose up`.
#
#   cp infra/.env.example infra/.env
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Every secret in docker-compose.yml is written as ${VAR:-some-default}, and
# those defaults are committed to this repository. That is convenient — the
# stack boots on a fresh clone with no setup — and it means that until you
# create infra/.env, every "secret" the application uses is public knowledge.
#
# The one that actually bites: INTEGRATION_ENCRYPTION_KEY encrypts BYOK API
# keys and webhook signing secrets at rest. Left at its default, those rows are
# encrypted with a key anyone who cloned the repo already has. "Encrypted at
# rest" becomes decorative.
#
# So: create infra/.env BEFORE storing any real credential through the app.
# Rotating afterwards permanently invalidates everything already encrypted
# (see the note on INTEGRATION_ENCRYPTION_KEY below).

# --- Application secrets ---
# Generate fresh values. Do not reuse the examples, and do not reuse one value
# across all three — they are separate secrets on purpose.
#
#   SECRET_KEY / JWT_SECRET_KEY:  openssl rand -hex 32
SECRET_KEY=
JWT_SECRET_KEY=

# base64 of exactly 32 bytes. core/encryption.py b64decodes this and asserts
# len == 32 for AES-256-GCM, so a hex string or a passphrase will fail at boot.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# ROTATION IS DESTRUCTIVE. Changing this makes every existing
# integrations.credentials blob and workflows.webhook_secret_encrypted value
# permanently undecryptable — AES-GCM raises InvalidTag rather than returning
# garbage. After rotating, re-enter each BYOK key through Settings and
# regenerate each workflow webhook secret.
INTEGRATION_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Cost controls ---
# Per-org daily cap on workflow runs, enforced in Redis before Celery enqueue.
# 0 disables it. Production default is 1000; keep it low in development, where
# a runaway retry loop is the realistic way to burn an LLM budget.
DAILY_RUN_QUOTA_PER_ORG=50

# --- OpenAI ---
# Optional. BYOK through the Settings page is the right path for application
# runs and is resolved per-organization at execution time.
#
# This variable is the documented fallback for seed scripts and probes, which
# call get_llm_client() with no organization to resolve a BYOK key against.
# Leave it empty unless you need those.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Infrastructure credentials ---
# These are read by the postgres and minio containers on FIRST BOOT ONLY and
# are then baked into their volumes. Changing them later without also removing
# the volumes produces authentication failures, not improved security.
# Change them before your first `docker compose up`, or not at all.
POSTGRES_USER=aap_user
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=aap_pass
POSTGRES_DB=aap_db
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin

# --- Optional observability ---
# LANGSMITH_API_KEY=
# SENTRY_DSN=

# --- Sign in with Google (optional) ----------------------------------------
# The button appears on /login only when BOTH are set. Redirect URI to authorize
# in Google Cloud: http://localhost:8000/api/v1/auth/google/callback
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
